---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import TableWrap from '../components/TableWrap.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Start a New Project" sectionLabel="New Projects" activeSlug="new-project">

  <section class="section" id="new-projects">
    <span class="section-label">New Projects</span>
    <h2>Using VibeCrew with New Projects</h2>
    <p>New projects follow the <strong>Two-Tier Workflow</strong>. Tier 1 creates the architectural foundation. Tier 2 builds features on top of it. The phase gate enforces this order &mdash; you cannot write source code until the foundation is complete.</p>

    <h3>Tier 1: The Project Foundation</h3>
    <p>Run <code>/new-project</code> to start a guided, 6-step process. Each step produces a concrete artifact before the next begins.</p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h4>VISION.md &mdash; Define project goals</h4>
        <p>VibeCrew asks: What problem does this solve? Who are the target users? What's the value proposition? What are the constraints? What does success look like?</p>
        <p>The output is <code>VISION.md</code> &mdash; a document every agent reads. When the Feature Developer writes code, it knows who your users are. When the Stack Scout evaluates technologies, it factors in your constraints.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Design Discovery &mdash; Establish visual identity</h4>
        <p>VibeCrew runs a <strong>3-phase design interview</strong> tailored to your product.
        First, it asks about your product category, audience, usage patterns, and emotional direction.
        Then it generates 3 visual directions specifically adapted to your answers &mdash; not generic options.
        Finally, it collects component preferences (navigation style, data display, interaction density)
        filtered by your earlier choices. The output: <code>design-system.css</code> (CSS custom properties)
        and <code>design-brief.md</code> (design rationale for agents to reference).
        <a href="design-discovery">Learn more about Design Discovery &rarr;</a></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>TDR &mdash; Research and lock the tech stack</h4>
        <p>The <strong>Stack Scout agent</strong> activates. This is a read-only research agent that runs in an <em>isolated context window</em>. It uses WebSearch, Context7, Chrome DevTools, and Supabase MCP (when available) to evaluate technology options against your requirements, then produces a <strong>Technology Decision Record</strong> at <code>docs/tdr-001-tech-stack.md</code>.</p>
        <p>The TDR documents what was considered, what was chosen, and why. Once approved, every agent works within these boundaries. The research costs 8,000&ndash;15,000 tokens but none of them touch your main session &mdash; only the distilled decision enters your context window.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3.5: Opponent Processor</strong> &mdash; Immediately after the TDR, VibeCrew invokes the Opponent Processor agent to stress-test every technology decision. You see both the TDR and the counter-analysis side by side, then choose: keep all decisions, reconsider specific ones, or skip. See <a href="#opponent">Opponent Processor</a> for details.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3.6: MCP Server Sync</strong> &mdash; After TDR approval, VibeCrew scans the TDR for technology mentions. Bundled servers (Supabase, Stripe, Vercel, Figma, Sentry, etc.) are auto-enabled. Additional servers from the registry (Firebase, Clerk, MongoDB, Netlify, and 11 more) are recommended if they match your TDR &mdash; you choose to add all, pick specific ones, or skip. You'll be prompted to set environment variables for authenticated servers. This is non-blocking &mdash; the workflow continues regardless.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Roadmap &mdash; Map out features</h4>
        <p>List your planned features in rough priority order. VibeCrew generates <code>docs/roadmap.md</code> with priority tiers (Must Have, Should Have, Nice to Have, Out of Scope). This becomes the input for <code>/plan-features</code> later.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Architecture Diagrams &mdash; Visualize the architecture</h4>
        <p>VibeCrew generates 5 Mermaid diagrams in <code>.vibecrew/architecture/</code> that give agents a compact visual architecture reference (~250&ndash;600 tokens for all 5, vs 2,000&ndash;5,000 for the full TDR):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>system.mmd</strong> &mdash; Infrastructure topology (services, boundaries, data flow)</li>
          <li><strong>schema.mmd</strong> &mdash; Database entity-relationship diagram</li>
          <li><strong>state-flows.mmd</strong> &mdash; Authentication states and primary user journeys</li>
          <li><strong>api-sequences.mmd</strong> &mdash; API request/response patterns</li>
          <li><strong>component-tree.mmd</strong> &mdash; Component hierarchy with data flow direction (extended during Tier 2)</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>CLAUDE.md &mdash; Codify project rules</h4>
        <p>VibeCrew synthesizes everything into a minimal rules file (~31 lines). It includes your tech stack, conventions, architecture rules, and a &ldquo;Session Learnings&rdquo; section (capped at 15 rules, oldest auto-pruned). Deliberately kept small &mdash; research shows oversized context files increase agent cost 20%+ without improving outcomes. No directory enumerations, no rules that hooks already enforce.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Git commit &mdash; Version the foundation</h4>
        <p>Everything is committed: <code>git commit -m "plan: project foundation"</code>. The phase gate unlocks. Source code writes are now allowed.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <Callout type="warn" title="The Phase Gate">
      Until all 6 steps are complete, a PreToolUse hook blocks all writes to source directories (src/, app/, lib/, components/). Configuration files (.vibecrew/, CLAUDE.md, docs/) are allowed. This is enforced by <code>phase-gate.sh</code> &mdash; it's a hard block, not a suggestion.
    </Callout>

    <h3>Tier 2: Building Features</h3>
    <p>With the foundation in place, you enter the iterative feature loop.</p>

    <h4>Planning the backlog</h4>
    <p>Run <code>/plan-features</code> to create structured specifications for each feature: name, description, problem statement, acceptance criteria, priority, complexity (S/M/L/XL), and dependencies. These are stored in <code>.vibecrew/backlog.json</code>.</p>

    <h4>Starting a feature</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">claude code</span>/new-feature "user authentication"</code></pre>
    <p>This creates a feature branch (<code>feat/user-authentication</code>), initializes 6-phase tracking, and loads the feature spec. You're now in the development loop.</p>

    <h4>The 6-phase cycle</h4>
    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">Plan</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">UI Design</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Code</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Test</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Review</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Docs</span>

<span class="muted">Phases can be worked in any order. Review is optional in manual workflows, automatic in /run-backlog.
Completeness is checked at /wrap, not during work.</span></div>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Plan</h4>
        <p>Problem statement, acceptance criteria, edge cases, and technical notes at <code>docs/features/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>UI Design</h4>
        <p>Component hierarchy, props, variants. Uses design system tokens. Mobile-first, WCAG AA compliant.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Code</h4>
        <p>Implementation within TDR boundaries. Checks Context7 before using any library API. Follows CLAUDE.md conventions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Test</h4>
        <p>TDD-hybrid: spec tests for business logic (written first), implementation tests for UI (written after). Tests behavior, not implementation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Review</h4>
        <p>Automated code review against feature spec, TDR compliance, project conventions, security, and performance. Findings ranked by severity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Docs</h4>
        <p>Feature documentation, CHANGELOG entries, session logs. Drift detection ensures docs stay current with code changes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Wrapping a session</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">claude code</span>/wrap</code></pre>
    <p>Checks completeness, runs the quality gate (tests + build + lint), triggers the Performance Coach for your Vibe Score, logs the session, and creates a conventional commit. <strong>Never close the terminal without wrapping.</strong></p>

    <h4>Automated execution</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">claude code</span>/run-backlog</code></pre>
    <p>Automatically picks the next planned feature by priority, executes it through Plan &rarr; Design &rarr; Code &rarr; Test, runs the quality gate, and loops. Stops on failure so you can fix and resume.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section">
    <h3>Phase gate blocks when it shouldn't</h3>
    <p>The phase gate message shows your progress: <em>"Foundation progress: 2/6 artifacts complete. Missing: tdr, roadmap, architecture diagrams, claude md."</em></p>
    <ol>
      <li>Check <code>.vibecrew/state.json</code> &mdash; is <code>foundation.complete</code> set to <code>true</code>?</li>
      <li>If any foundation artifacts are incomplete, run <code>/new-project</code> to complete the missing steps or <code>/status</code> for details</li>
      <li>The phase gate allows writes to config paths (<code>.vibecrew/</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>docs/</code>). If a legitimate config file is blocked, check the path patterns in <code>phase-gate.sh</code></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
